serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>The World Cup of diplomacy</span></h2><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span s=
tyle=3D"font-weight:700">Farnaz, I usually call you up to talk about Iran, =
but you=E2=80=99re also our U.N. bureau chief and have been covering these =
annual meetings for a decade. People love to discuss how irrelevant the U.N=
=2E has become. How much does the U.N. still matter=3F</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I=
t=E2=80=99s the only forum in the world that brings countries =E2=80=94 all=
 193 of them =E2=80=94 together under one roof. They call it the World Cup =
of diplomacy. Delegations attend bilateral meetings, group meetings, themat=
ic meetings on things like rising ocean levels and food security. These are=
 issues that need a space for discussion, and the U.N. is really the only p=
lace that offers that.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,=
serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>What comes out that=E2=80=99s actio=
nable=3F Very little. But it moves the conversation forward. And there=E2=
=80=99s value to that if you believe in diplomacy. We=E2=80=99re so used to=
 thinking, =E2=80=9COh, the U.N. Security Council is so divided; the U.S. a=
nd China and Russia can=E2=80=99t agree on anything.=E2=80=9D But as one U.=
N. expert pointed out, when you compare it to the Cold War days, when the U=
=2ES. and the Soviet Union were at loggerheads, the U.N. is still doing a l=
ot more today than it was then.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px=
 georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">=
